        "denialFound": "The Ministry of Defence's position appears inside the same PRS PDF (p.2), citing the Standing Committee report: \"According to the Ministry of Defence, spending 3% of India's GDP on the defence sector will lead to much higher allocations which the defence services may not be able to absorb.\" This is the Ministry's own documented response on the 3% benchmark. No denial of the share figures exists, and none is needed because they are arithmetic from budget documents.",
        "innocentReading": "The defence budget is growing in absolute terms (9% a year) while total central spending grows faster (10% a year), so defence's share falls without any cut. The Ministry itself says the services could not absorb 3% of GDP. The GDP ratio counts MoD pensions; leaving pensions out lowers it by about 0.5 percentage points. It also excludes MHA's paramilitary forces (CAPFs).",
