        "denialFound": "At the hearing, Solicitor General Tushar Mehta replied to the CJI's remark on non-cooperation: \"I am not aware of that\" (LiveLaw and Alt News, https://www.altnews.in/amit-malviyas-claim-that-the-sc-did-not-allege-non-cooperation-by-the-govt-during-pegasus-probe-is-misleading/). BJP IT cell head Amit Malviya publicly said the SC \"hasn't alleged non cooperation by central Govt\". Alt News rated this misleading, but confirmed the written order of 25 Aug 2022 is procedural only and says nothing on cooperation. The non-cooperation finding exists only as the CJI's oral reading of the sealed report. The government did not issue a substantive rebuttal of the 5-of-29 finding. It has held throughout that national-security details cannot be disclosed.",
        "innocentReading": "Finding generic malware on 5 of 29 phones does not show state surveillance. The committee reportedly said it could not attribute the malware to Pegasus, and it may reflect poor cyber hygiene. The government's \"non-cooperation\" matches the stand it took openly in court: it refused to say on affidavit, on national-security grounds, whether it used Pegasus. It is not necessarily evidence of concealment. The claim as worded (\"Pegasus not established\") already avoids both over-readings: \"Pegasus was used\" and \"Pegasus was disproven\".",
