        "denialFound": "None found. I did not ask. Neither Chander, Major, Astra Microwave nor Reliance was asked, and no outlet has put an allegation to them. A search found no reporting that alleges impropriety in either appointment (Fali Major search: Wikipedia, Bharat Rakshak, a Business Standard report on the R-Infra takeover of Pipavav; no controversy about the board seat). With no allegation there is nothing to deny, and that absence is the record.",
        "innocentReading": "Listed defence-electronics and shipbuilding firms are expected by SEBI LODR to fill independent-director seats with people who have relevant expertise, and the pool of people with senior missile, radar or air-acquisition experience is almost entirely retired officers and DRDO scientists. Astra's skills matrix lists Chander's DRDO background as the reason for his seat. Both appointments came years after retirement, outside the cooling-off rule. Chander had been removed early by the same government, which does not fit a \"reward\" from government. The real issue the narrative points at is lobbying and middlemen. The documented source for that is the 2012 Standing Committee statement quoted by PTI, which is generic and names no one.",
        "reason": "The claim's causal core (\"as a reward or for access\") has no source. None of the four sources says any retired officer or scientist got a board seat as a reward or for access. The PTI/Shillong Times piece quotes the Standing Committee: \"some of the retired defence officers work with the private sector and act as a middlemen for defence procurement\". That is a generic concern, names no one, and does not support the reward-or-access mechanism. Antony also said \"Retired officers of the army or bureaucrats work for Indian companies, foreign companies\". The two named cases both fall outside the cooling-off window, there is no base rate, and the DPSU and UPA-II controls were not run. On top of that, the strongestCase overstates its evidence. It labels the Air Chief's board seat \"Documented\", but the record's own claim c029 rates it \"reported\" (Wikipedia only, with the annual report not opened). It uses the present tense (\"sits\") for a board that was reconstituted in Dec 2023. And it dates Chander's chairmanship from his appointment as a director. The entry should stay as an analytic narrative, with its status lowered and its wording fixed. It does not belong at any evidentiary tier above analytic. Killing it is not needed: it is a real circulating concern with a documented parliamentary origin. But because no one has alleged anything against Chander or Major, the narrative should not lead with them as its illustration.",
