        "denialFound": "None found, not asked. The MoD's replies printed inside CAG Reports 4 of 2007 and 3 of 2019 were not opened because cag.gov.in was unreachable. The ORF author makes no accusation: the article frames the issue as a reform problem (QR drafting, too many approval points, accountability) and never calls procurement rigged.",
        "innocentReading": "Army staff wrote qualitative requirements that were unrealistic, could not be tested or did not match the global market, so only one vendor's product qualified. On top of that, many weapons have only one or two makers worldwide, and government-to-government (IGA/FMS) deals are single-vendor by design. That produces high single-vendor shares without any favour to a vendor. The CAG's own explanation (as ORF quotes it) is drafting, not collusion.",
        "reason": "The core verdict holds: \"contested\", with \"rigged\" unsupported. The one source opened (ORF, Behera, February 2021) quotes the CAG's 60% single-vendor finding and puts it down to badly drafted QRs. Neither the article nor the audit it quotes names a beneficiary or a mechanism of favour, and the base rate (US DoD about 43% not competed) shows high single-vendor shares are normal in defence buying. But the summary text is wrong in three ways. (1) \"prevalence documented 2007\" is the wrong tier: the CAG report was not opened, so the figure is reported via ORF, not documented. (2) The prevalence is for Indian Army procurements, not defence procurement as a whole. (3) The strongestCounter says the CAG blamed QRs \"not favour\", yet the same ORF article quotes CAG Report 3 of 2019 finding IAF QRs \"vendor-driven, and subject to repeated changes during the procurement process\" with \"unwarranted specifications\". That is the strongest evidence for the claim, and leaving it out tilts the balance. With these fixes the narrative should stay as a contested, analytic rating.",
