        "denialFound": "Yes: the government denied the financial motive, and both cited sources record it. (1) Rajnath Singh, June 2022 PIB press briefing, quoted in ORF IB 567 (https://www.orfonline.org/research/the-case-for-agnipath): \"We never see the army from the point of view of savings.\" (2) The Wire, \"Why Is the Military Disowning Agnipath's Pension Saving Element?\" (https://thewire.in/security/agnipath-scheme-pension-savings-military-denial) reports that senior officers of all three services and the DMA under Lt Gen Anil Puri declared pension savings were \"most definitely\" not a consideration. Per the search-result summary, Puri also said his analysis had \"no financial chapter\". (3) DPG (https://www.delhipolicygroup.org/publication/policy-briefs/the-agnipath-scheme-analysis-and-a-way-forward.html) says \"the government and the military are silent on the issue of manpower savings.\"",
        "innocentReading": "Agnipath really does serve both aims, and ORF's own words are \"equally driven\". The youth-profile aim (average age from 32 to 24–26, in line with peer armies) is real. On ORF's arithmetic the pension savings only arrive after 2044 (\"there will be no savings in the near to medium term\"), so leading with youth and capability is a defensible description of the near-term effect, not necessarily a cover story. A government may also avoid citing pension savings for political reasons, because of protests and veterans' sensitivity, without the reform being a pretext.",
