        "denialFound": "No denial from a party to the claim was found. The UN has not been recorded confirming or denying the 222/1:450 norm: Africa Check asked, and \"The UN did not respond to our queries about if they have any ratio\" (https://www.polity.org.za/article/south-african-police-minister-cites-dubious-un-standard-as-he-calls-for-60-000-more-officers-2019-07-02). Africa Check's follow-up is headlined \"Still no UN recommended police-to-population ratio\" (https://africacheck.org/fact-checks/spotchecks/still-no-un-recommended-police-population-ratio-ghost-statistic-cited-again). That page returned 403 here and was not opened. MHA was not asked by us. A 2021 Rajya Sabha unstarred answer by MoS G. Kishan Reddy gave sanctioned 195.39 and actual 155.78 per lakh in reply to a question that cited UN recommendations; that answer was not opened here.",
        "innocentReading": "155 per lakh is a real, documented low ratio by international comparison, and vacancies against India's own sanctioned norms are real. But \"222\" is a ghost statistic that Indian policy writing (PRS 2017, the Standing Committee, the press) has repeated as a UN standard without a UN instrument behind it. Low ratios in large, populous states may also reflect how much a state budget can pay for, and the federal division of policing (a State List subject). They do not by themselves show that India is \"dangerously\" under-policed. No Indian elasticity linking police numbers to crime was opened, and Chalfin & McCrary's estimate is for US cities only.",
